{
"Asked how it could be bliss when nothing is felt, he said that was exactly why.": "40e9e787a750",
"Epicurus also defined the goal by absence: no pain in the body, no trouble in the soul.": "a3745fcc3688",
"Epicurus defined the highest good negatively, as nirvana is defined. In R. D. Hicks translation of the Letter to Menoeceus: By pleasure we mean the absence of pain in the body and of trouble in the soul. It is reached not by indulgence but by sober reasoning, searching out the grounds of every choice and avoidance, and banishing those beliefs through which the greatest tumults take possession of the soul. Most striking is his teaching on death. Accustom thyself to believe that death is nothing to us, because death is the end of sentience, and so a right understanding that death is nothing to us makes the mortality of life enjoyable, not by adding to life an illimitable time, but by taking away the yearning after immortality. The Buddha also cured the craving for continued existence. The difference is the picture of death. For Epicurus death is simple nonexistence, and he offered this as comfort. The Buddha refused that description of a realized one, and the Yamaka sutta calls it a harmful misconception.": "e808edc8d264",
"For Aristotle the highest good is eudaimonia, and in the last book of the Nicomachean Ethics he identifies its best form with contemplation. In W. D. Rosss translation: If happiness is activity in accordance with virtue, it is reasonable that it should be in accordance with the highest virtue; and this will be that of the best thing in us. Contemplation is the most self-sufficient activity, since the philosopher, even when by himself, can contemplate truth. Then comes a famous exhortation: we must, so far as we can, make ourselves immortal, and strain every nerve to live in accordance with the best thing in us. The Buddhist and the Aristotelian ideals share the value of self-sufficiency, a life that does not depend on fortune, and both place it in the mind. They point in opposite directions, though. Aristotles happiness is the fullest activity of the best part of a person, a self raised to its highest power. The Buddhas goal is the end of the fires, and it is not the perfection of a faculty. Aristotle asks us to make ourselves immortal, while Epicurus and the Buddha both ask us to drop the yearning.": "f9ddaff3a608",
"Is it happiness? Extinguishment is the ultimate happiness, says the Dhammapada. But when Sāriputta exclaims, Reverends, extinguishment is bliss! a monk objects: Whats blissful about it, since nothing is felt? Sāriputta answers, The fact that nothing is felt is precisely whats blissful about it (Aṅguttara Nikāya 9.34). The happiness of nirvana is the end of being driven, and it is not a pleasant feeling of a higher kind. The Itivuttaka distinguishes two aspects. In a living arahant, their five sense faculties still remain and they still feel pleasure and pain, but greed, hate, and delusion have ended. That is extinguishment with residue. At death, everything thats felt, being no longer relished, will become cool right here, which is extinguishment with no residue (Itivuttaka 44).": "7a1b87bbc260",
"Nirvana means a fire going out. The fires are greed, hatred, and delusion.": "3668407e3584",
"Nāgārjuna said there is not the slightest difference between nirvana and the world.": "0d6d1944f336",
"Spinozas Ethics ends with an account of the highest good that resembles nirvana in several ways. In R. H. M. Elwes translation: Blessedness is not the reward of virtue, but virtue itself; neither do we rejoice therein, because we control our lusts, but, contrariwise, because we rejoice therein, we are able to control our lusts. The wise man is scarcely at all disturbed in spirit and always possesses true acquiescence of his spirit, and the way there is understanding. The ignorant man, by contrast, is distracted in various ways by external causes. Then the famous last line: all things excellent are as difficult as they are rare. Like nirvana, Spinozas blessedness is not a prize for good behavior, and it comes through seeing things as they are. The difference lies in what is seen. Spinozas wise man understands himself and all things as part of God, and his blessedness is love towards God. The Buddhist sees that nothing, including the self, has a fixed nature to be loved or held.": "81cdb831fe5c",
"Sāriputta said, The ending of greed, hate, and delusion is called extinguishment.": "7b6b66ef38be",
"The Buddha would not say that the awakened are annihilated, or that they survive as a soul.": "a7dce8ff82e5",
"The closest is Epicurus. His goal is defined by absence, the absence of pain and trouble, as nirvana is defined by the ending of three fires. He reaches it by removing false beliefs, and he treats the craving for immortality as a disease to be cured. Spinoza is closest on the relation between the goal and the path, since blessedness is not the reward of virtue, but virtue itself, and it comes through understanding. Aristotle shares the ideal of a self-sufficient life of the mind and points it the other way, toward an activity that makes us as immortal as we can be.": "586b6238dace",
"The first further step is to take the self out of the highest good. Western ethics, from Aristotle to Spinoza, asks what the best life is for a person, and assumes there is a person to whom the good belongs. The Buddha asked what remains when the fires that build the sense of a person go out, and he declined to name it in terms of a self. Sāriputtas answer to Yamaka is the key. Even now, a realized one cannot be found in or apart from the five aggregates. So annihilated after death and survives after death both mistake what was there to begin with. This is hard for Western listeners, and it should not be made easier by calling nirvana a state of the soul or a void. The honest statement is that the tradition describes it by what ends, by its peace, and by its reality, and declines to describe it as a possession.": "40c4ca88ed4c",
"The likeness ends at what remains. Each Western view needs a subject to enjoy the good: Epicurus untroubled soul, Aristotles contemplating intellect, Spinozas mind that loves God. The Buddhist goal has no such subject. Nirvana is not something the self gets, and the suttas will not describe it as the selfs survival or its destruction. This is why Western readers have alternately called nirvana nihilism and mysticism, and why both labels miss. Nāgārjuna pressed the point furthest: Between nirvana and the world there is not the slightest difference. Nirvana is not another place. It is this same world, seen and lived without the fires.": "3b1f21dd5f21",
"The second step is that nirvana is not elsewhere. Popular Western images, and some popular Buddhist ones, picture nirvana as a distant heaven or a final exit. The suttas already say that the arahant with residue lives in the world, still seeing and hearing, with the fires out. Nāgārjuna went further in the Middle Treatise. Between nirvana and the world there is not the slightest difference, and the limit of nirvana and the limit of the world: between these two there is not the slightest hairs breadth of difference. The commentary explains that because all things are without arising and ceasing, the world and nirvana cannot be told apart. This is where Mahāyāna teaching on the bodhisattva, who stays in the world out of compassion, finds its ground. For a teacher, the practical point is that nirvana is described as a change in how one lives here, and not as a journey to somewhere else.": "d116f493600c",
"The word nibbāna means the going out of a fire, and the suttas define it by what ends. A wanderer asks Sāriputta, What is extinguishment? In Bhikkhu Sujatos translation the answer is: Reverend, the ending of greed, hate, and delusion is called extinguishment (Saṁyutta Nikāya 38.1). The Chinese Connected Discourses give the same exchange: Nirvana is the permanent ending of greed, the permanent ending of hatred, the permanent ending of delusion, the permanent ending of all defilements. And is there a path to it? There is: the noble eightfold path. The goal is not a reward handed out at the end of a road. It is the condition of a mind in which three fires have gone out.": "3cfc993493eb",
"Two misreadings appear in the suttas themselves. The first is that nirvana is annihilation. A monk named Yamaka held that a mendicant who has ended the defilements is annihilated and destroyed when their body breaks up. The other monks call this a harmful misconception, and Sāriputta shows him that even in this life a realized one cannot be found in or apart from the five aggregates (Saṁyutta Nikāya 22.85). If he cannot be located now, it makes no sense to say that something is destroyed later. The second misreading is that nirvana is nothing at all. The Udāna answers: There is, mendicants, freedom from rebirth, freedom from what has been produced, made, and conditioned. If there were no freedom you would find no escape here (Udāna 8.3). Nirvana is real, and it is not a thing among things, a self that survives, or a heaven.": "572ebb2cc1a9",
"Westerners usually meet nirvana as a word for bliss, or as a rock band, or as a polite name for oblivion. Begin with the word itself: it means a fire going out, and the fires are greed, hatred, and delusion. Then give Sāriputtas two answers. Asked what nirvana is, he said the ending of those three. Asked how it can be bliss when nothing is felt, he said that is exactly why. For people who know some philosophy, Epicurus is the best bridge, because he too defined the goal as an absence and wanted to cure the yearning for immortality. Then name the difference plainly. The Buddha would not call the awakened annihilated, and he would not call them a surviving soul. End with Nāgārjunas line, because it keeps nirvana from sounding like an escape.": "facc1a43a619"
}
